# Maps.
#
# Three primitives, and not one of them returns a collection.
# `mapLength` and `mapAt` because a map cannot be taken apart from inside
# the language, and `mapKeyAt` because a fold needs something to walk.
#
# WHAT MADE THE SECOND HALF OF THIS FILE WRITABLE. `set key to value in
# table`, the construction form. This file used to open by observing
# that not one of its three primitives returned a collection, and that
# was the whole of why `mapRemove`, `mapMerge`, `mapValues` and `mapOf`
# could not be written: each of them has to hand a map back, and nothing
# in the language made one. `append item to list` is the same sentence
# for lists, and `list.zd` records the day it landed.
#
# ONE FORM AND NOT TWO. The obvious pair was "this map with one key set"
# and "this map without one key", and the second is not here. A map is
# immutable, so every construction copies the whole map anyway, so a
# removal written as a fold above `set … in` costs the same order as a
# native delete on a copy: O(n) either way. `mapRemove` below is that
# fold. A second form would have bought a smaller constant and one more
# thing for the language to mean, which is the trade §4.1 refuses. The
# rejected alternative is recorded rather than merely not taken.
#
# WHAT MADE THE REST OF THIS FILE WRITABLE. `mapKeyAt` — the key at a
# position, or `None` past the end. It is the map's `listAt`, and it is
# the smallest thing that closes the gap: `mapAt` answers a question about
# a key you already have, `mapLength` gives a count with no way to turn a
# count into a key, and a map has no other operation at all. So an
# enumeration has to be primitive; the only choice was how much of one.
# `keys of` was the most — a whole collection, built by the platform, in
# an order the language then had to take on trust. `mapKeyAt` is the
# least: one key, addressed the one way the language already addresses a
# sequence, and every collection over a map is built above it in
# ZDeceptron.
#
# ORDER. A key's position is its insertion order, and that is a promise
# rather than an observation. The runtime value of a `Map of K to V` is a
# JavaScript `Map` and never an object (§5.4), and a `Map` enumerates in
# insertion order for every kind of key where an object does not, for
# integer-like ones. Every mutation builds a new map rather than writing
# into the old one, so a map's order is settled when it is built:
# `["a" to 1, "b" to 2]`, the pair form `[["a",1],["b",2]]` it serialises
# to, and the map rebuilt from those pairs all enumerate `a` then `b`. A
# durable map that came back in a different order would be a build that
# is not reproducible.
#
# The construction form keeps that promise rather than merely not
# breaking it. `set key to value in table` emits `new Map(m).set(k, v)`:
# `new Map(m)` copies in iteration order, and `set` on a key the copy
# already holds replaces the value and leaves the key where it was. So a
# new key goes on the end and an existing key does not move, which is
# what every function below relies on to rebuild a map in the order it
# read one.
#
# COST. `mapKeyAt` is O(1) per call, so every fold below is linear in the
# size of the map: `keys of` and `values of` are O(n), and `keyOfOr` is
# O(n) and stops at the first match. Each builds with `append` in tail
# position, which is the only shape that stays linear — `list.zd` records
# why.
#
# A fold that builds a *map* is the one exception, and it is quadratic:
# `set … in` copies, so `mapRemove`, `mapMerge` and `mapOf` are each O(n²)
# in the number of entries. That is the honest cost of an immutable map
# with no chain, and it is written down here rather than discovered. A
# caller rebuilding a large map repeatedly should build it once.
#
# `atOr` is the operation `examples/leaderboard.zd` could not be written
# without: `sort each player by atOr with table is table, key is
# player.name, fallback is 0`.

foreign mapLength is anywhere
    from "zd:map" as "length"
    takes of value is Map of K to V
    gives pure Whole

foreign mapAt is anywhere
    from "zd:map" as "at"
    takes value is Map of K to V, key is K
    gives pure Option of V

foreign mapKeyAt is anywhere
    from "zd:map" as "keyAt"
    takes value is Map of K to V, index is Whole
    gives pure Option of K

# The keys, in insertion order.
#
# This was a `foreign` until `mapKeyAt` existed, and the reason was not
# the one §17.4.10 gave. Construction had stopped being a blocker when
# `append item to list` landed; what a fold still had no way to get was a
# sequence to walk, and the only sequence a map offered was this
# function's own answer. Writing `keys of` needed exactly the operation
# `keys of` is. It does not any more.
function keys of table
    give keysFrom with table is table, index is 0, taken is empty

function keysFrom with table, index, taken
    when mapKeyAt with value is table, index is index
        None
            give taken
        Some with key
            give keysFrom with table is table, index is index + 1, taken is (append key to taken)

# The values, each in the position of the key it belongs to.
#
# It walks the entries rather than the keys. It used to build the whole
# key list first and walk that, which cost a second collection to produce
# the one that was asked for.
function values of table
    give valuesFrom with table is table, index is 0, taken is empty

function valuesFrom with table, index, taken
    when mapKeyAt with value is table, index is index
        None
            give taken
        Some with key
            when mapAt with value is table, key is key
                None
                    give valuesFrom with table is table, index is index + 1, taken is taken
                Some with value
                    give valuesFrom with table is table, index is index + 1, taken is (append value to taken)

# The target of `table contains key` when `table` is a Map.
function mapContains with table, key
    give isSome of (mapAt with value is table, key is key)

function atOr with table, key, fallback
    give valueOr with maybe is (mapAt with value is table, key is key), fallback is fallback

# A map read backwards: the first key holding `value`, or `fallback` when
# no key holds it.
#
# The fold that needs both halves of an entry at once, and therefore the
# one that shows what walking a map buys beyond walking a list. It takes
# a `fallback` rather than giving an `Option` because a `choice` value
# cannot be written in expression position — the same reason `atOr` sits
# next to `mapAt`.
function keyOfOr with table, value, fallback
    give keyOfFrom with table is table, value is value, fallback is fallback, index is 0

function keyOfFrom with table, value, fallback, index
    when mapKeyAt with value is table, index is index
        None
            give fallback
        Some with key
            when mapAt with value is table, key is key
                None
                    give keyOfFrom with table is table, value is value, fallback is fallback, index is index + 1
                Some with held
                    if held is value
                        give key
                    give keyOfFrom with table is table, value is value, fallback is fallback, index is index + 1

# --- the functions that hand a map back -----------------------------------
#
# None of these could be written before `set key to value in table`, and
# every one of them is an ordinary ZDeceptron fold now. The primitive
# layer did not grow: `zdc-lib`'s foreign count is unchanged.

# Every entry, as a list of pairs, in the order the map enumerates.
#
# The projection `keys of` and `values of` could not make between them:
# each loses the other half, and a caller wanting both had to walk the
# map twice and trust the two walks to agree. A pair is the return type
# this needed, and not having one is why the issue was open.
function entries of table
    give entriesFrom with table is table, index is 0, taken is empty

function entriesFrom with table, index, taken
    when mapKeyAt with value is table, index is index
        None
            give taken
        Some with key
            when mapAt with value is table, key is key
                None
                    give entriesFrom with table is table, index is index + 1, taken is taken
                Some with value
                    give entriesFrom with table is table, index is index + 1, taken is (append (Pair with first is key, second is value) to taken)

# A map holding every pair of `pairs`, in the order they are written.
#
# The direction back in. `mapOf of (entries of table)` is `table`, keys
# and order alike, which is the round trip the wire format already
# depends on. A repeated key keeps its first position and takes its last
# value, which is what `set … in` means one entry at a time.
function mapOf of pairs
    give mapOfFrom with pairs is pairs, index is 0, taken is empty

function mapOfFrom with pairs, index, taken
    when listAt with value is pairs, index is index
        None
            give taken
        Some with entry
            give mapOfFrom with pairs is pairs, index is index + 1, taken is (set entry.first to entry.second in taken)

# `table` without `key`. A key the map does not hold removes nothing.
#
# Named `mapRemove` and not `remove` because `remove` is a keyword: it is
# §14B.2's mutation, and the mutation works on a place, which is exactly
# why removing a key from a *derived* map had no form. `listContains`
# carries a prefix for the same kind of reason.
#
# It rebuilds rather than deleting, so the keys that stay keep the order
# they had. Equality is `is`, the same equality `contains` uses, so the
# key type is one `is` can answer for.
function mapRemove with table, key
    give mapRemoveFrom with table is table, key is key, index is 0, taken is empty

function mapRemoveFrom with table, key, index, taken
    when mapKeyAt with value is table, index is index
        None
            give taken
        Some with held
            if held is key
                give mapRemoveFrom with table is table, key is key, index is index + 1, taken is taken
            when mapAt with value is table, key is held
                None
                    give mapRemoveFrom with table is table, key is key, index is index + 1, taken is taken
                Some with value
                    give mapRemoveFrom with table is table, key is key, index is index + 1, taken is (set held to value in taken)

# `base` with every entry of `extra` written over it.
#
# `extra` wins where both hold a key, which is the direction that makes
# `mapMerge with base is defaults, extra is chosen` read the way it looks.
# A key `base` already holds keeps its position and takes `extra`'s
# value; a key only `extra` holds arrives at the end, in `extra`'s order.
function mapMerge with base, extra
    give mapMergeFrom with base is base, extra is extra, index is 0

function mapMergeFrom with base, extra, index
    when mapKeyAt with value is extra, index is index
        None
            give base
        Some with key
            when mapAt with value is extra, key is key
                None
                    give mapMergeFrom with base is base, extra is extra, index is index + 1
                Some with value
                    give mapMergeFrom with base is (set key to value in base), extra is extra, index is index + 1

# `table`'s keys, each holding the value at the same position in
# `values`.
#
# The key-preserving transform, in the one shape the language allows.
# There are no first-class functions (§17.2), so nothing here can take a
# transform; the new values arrive already computed, exactly as `anyOf`
# takes truths rather than a predicate:
#
#   mapValues with table is scores,
#             values is (from values of scores map each s to s * 2)
#
# That is sound rather than a hope, and `values of` is why: it walks the
# entries in the order `keys of` walks the keys, so position i of one
# belongs to position i of the other. Written out, this is `zip` between
# those two lists and `mapOf` over the result, so it is spelled that way
# rather than folded a fourth time.
#
# A `values` list shorter than the map drops the keys past its end,
# because `zip` stops at the shorter side.
function mapValues with table, values
    give mapOf of (zip with left is (keys of table), right is values)
